{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfbfQ52uxdZG2espzZECr", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "A list is mutable and a tuple is immutable. Most other differences follow from that.\n\n| | **List** | **Tuple** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Syntax | `[1, 2, 3]` | `(1, 2, 3)` or `1, 2, 3` |\n| Mutable | Yes. You can append, remove, or reassign items. | No. Items can't be changed after creation. |\n| Hashable | No, so it can't be a dict key or set member. | Yes, if all its items are hashable. |\n| Methods | Many (`append`, `extend`, `pop`, `sort`, \u2026) | Only `count` and `index` |\n| Memory and speed | Slightly more overhead | Slightly smaller and faster to create |\n| Typical use | A homogeneous collection that grows or changes | A fixed record of related values, such as `(x, y)` or `(name, age)` |\n\n```python\nlst = [1, 2, 3]\nlst[0] = 99          # OK\nlst.append(4)        # OK\n\ntup = (1, 2, 3)\ntup[0] = 99          # TypeError: 'tuple' object does not support item assignment\n\nd = {(1, 2): \"point\"}   # tuple as dict key works\nd = {[1, 2]: \"point\"}   # TypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\n```\n\n**Gotchas**\n- A single-item tuple needs a trailing comma: `(5,)`. Without it, `(5)` is just the integer 5.\n- Tuple immutability is shallow. If a tuple contains a list, you can still mutate that list: `t = ([1], 2); t[0].append(3)` works.\n\n**Rule of thumb:** use a tuple for a fixed group of values that belong together, and a list for a collection you expect to modify."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7212, "cache_read_input_tokens": 16780, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7212}, "output_tokens": 1, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management"